{"records":[{"id":"fmxdjgrjw5","title":"Genetically Encoded and Modular SubCellular Organelle Probes (GEM-SCOPe) reveal lysosomal and mitochondrial dysfunction driven by PRKN knockout","description":"Raw imaging data for \"Genetically Encoded and Modular SubCellular Organelle Probes (GEM-SCOPe) reveal lysosomal and mitochondrial dysfunction driven by PRKN knockout\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-29T14:42:56.132Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fmxdjgrjw5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fmxdjgrjw5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Camille","last_name":"Goldman","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03zj4c476","name":"Aligning Science Across Parkinson's","grant_id":"ASAP-024297"},{"id":"https://ror.org/05ewr7t48","name":"Cure Alzheimer’s Fund","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/027ka1x80","name":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration","grant_id":"80ARC022CA004"},{"id":"https://ror.org/049v75w11","name":"National Institute on Aging","grant_id":"T32AG04968"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04hf5kq57","name":"New York State Department of Health","grant_id":"NYSTEM-C32561GG"},{"id":"https://ror.org/049v75w11","name":"National Institute on Aging","grant_id":"F31NS13090"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"R01NS114239"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01s5ya894","name":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grant_id":"UH3NS115064"}],"categories":["Neurodegenerative Disorder","Parkinson's Disease","Induced Pluripotent Stem Cell","Fluorescence Imaging","Lentiviral Vector","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yhjnbnsgz3","title":"Generation of a mitochondrial protein compendium in Dictyostelium discoideum","description":"The data file contains 10 original microscopy image files, Related to Figure 3, and 10 excel files (Supplemental Tables 1-10).\n\nSupplemental Table descriptions:\nTable S1. D. discoideum homologs of human mitochondrial proteins, Related to Figure 1.\nTable S2. Proteomic discovery relative enrichments and relative enrichment ratios, Related to Figure 1. \nTable S3. Proteomic discovery core mitochondrial proteins, Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4. Predicted probabilities of mitochondrial localization based on GMM, Related to Figure 2. \nTable S5. Proteomic discovery mitochondrial targeting sequence prediction, Related to Figure 2. \nTable S6. Microscopy validation of relative enrichment ratio cutoff, Related to Figure 3. \nTable S7. Classifications of the mitochondrial protein compendium, Related to Tables 1 and 2.\nTable S8. All ribosomal proteins in the mitochondrial protein compendium, Related to Table 2.\nTable S9. Expression profiles for genes encoding mitochondrial proteins, Related to Figure 4.  \nTable S10. List of oligos used for cloning, Related to Figure 3. \n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-27T20:20:27.317Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yhjnbnsgz3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yhjnbnsgz3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Freitas","orcid":"0000-0001-5540-2750"},{"first_name":"Jake","last_name":"Herb","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Miao","last_name":"Pan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marjan","last_name":"Gucek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tian","last_name":"Jin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Metabolism","Dictyostelium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tkfmmx45y2","title":"EhSSP2-mediated hijacking of the host APP/Aβ pathway drives biphasic apoptosis in Microsporidia infection","description":"Microsporidia are obligate intracellular parasites that pose a significant threat to both immunocompetent and immunocompromised individuals, with current therapies lacking effectiveness against crucial lifecycle stages. In this study, we identify a novel sporoplasm surface protein, EhSSP2, from Encephalitozoon hellem, which directly interacts with the host amyloid precursor protein (APP) through conserved GXXXG motifs, specifically targeting APP's amyloid-β (Aβ) domain. This interaction establishes a functional SSP2-APP-VDAC1-SSP1 signaling axis, whereby EhSSP2 enhances BACE1 expression, promoting APP cleavage and subsequent Aβ production, thereby linking to mitochondrial VDAC1. Importantly, E. hellem exhibits biphasic regulation of apoptosis. Early suppression of apoptosis occurs to facilitate intracellular replication, followed by activation during later stages to induce mitochondrial dysfunction and promote spore dissemination. Pharmacological validation confirms that apoptosis is essential for efficient spore release. Our findings reveal a novel strategy by which microsporidia hijack the host APP/Aβ pathway for their propagation, providing mechanistic insights into organelle hijacking and potential therapeutic targets for microsporidiosis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T12:02:11.504Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tkfmmx45y2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tkfmmx45y2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong-Liang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6k3ft58vb2","title":"Supplementary Data to the Dissertation \"Proximity biotinylation at organelle contact sites reveals GRAMD1B as a sterol transporter acting at the lysosome-ER interface\"","description":"Supplementary data to the work presented in the dissertation by Valentin Schoop with the Title \"Proximity biotinylation at organelle contact sites reveals GRAMD1B as a sterol transporter acting at the lysosome-ER interface\"\nGroup of Dr. Doris Höglinger. Submitted in March 2022 at Heidelberg University","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-18T08:32:02.469Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6k3ft58vb2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6k3ft58vb2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Valentin","last_name":"Schoop","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Mass Spectrometry","Plasmid","Image Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dntgsyznzs","title":"The genetic background shapes disease susceptibility and reveals a specific role for mitochondrial dysfunction in the progression from NAFLD to NASH","description":"Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) is a global health concern without treatment. The challenge in finding effective therapies is due to the lack of good mouse models and the complexity of the disease, characterized by gene-environment interactions. We tested the susceptibility of 7 mouse strains to develop NASH. The severity of the clinical phenotypes observed varied widely across strains. PWK/PhJ mice were the most prone to develop NASH, while CAST/EiJ mice were completely resistant. Levels of transcripts and proteins present in the mitochondria as well as mitochondrial function were robustly reduced in the liver of PWK/PhJ mice, suggesting a central role of mitochondrial dysfunction in NASH progression. Importantly, the alterations in gene expression observed in PWK/PhJ mice were the closest to the human NASH. Our study exposes the limitations of using a single mouse genetic background in metabolic studies and describes a novel NASH mouse model that closely resembles the human disease. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-09T13:53:02.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dntgsyznzs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dntgsyznzs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Giorgia","last_name":"Benegiamo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences","Mitochondrion","Metabolism","Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pdmp3zj27v","title":"NEMF-mediated Listerin-independent mitochondrial translational surveillance by E3 ligase Pirh2 and mitochondrial protease ClpXP","description":"The ribosome-associated protein quality control (RQC) pathway acts as a translational surveillance mechanism to maintain proteostasis. In mammalian cells, the cytoplasmic RQC pathway involves Nuclear Export Mediator Factor (NEMF)-dependent recruitment of the E3 ligase, Listerin, to ubiquitinate ribosome-stalled nascent polypeptides on the lysine residue for degradation. However, the quality control of ribosome-stalled  nuclear-encoded mitochondrial nascent polypeptide remains elusive, as these peptides can be partially imported into mitochondrion through translocon, restricting accessibility to the lysine by Listerin. Here we identified a Listerin-independent organelle-specific mitochondrial RQC pathway that acts on NEMF-mediated carboxyl terminal poly-alanine modification. In the pathway, mitochondrial proteins carrying C-end poly-Ala tails are recognized by cytosolic E3 ligase Pirh2 and by the ClpXP protease in the mitochondria, which coordinately clear ribosome-stalled mitochondrial nascent polypeptides. Defects in this elimination pathway result in NEMF-mediated aggregates and mitochondrial integrity failure, thus providing a potential molecular mechanism of RQC pathway in mitochondrial-associated human diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-29T21:38:13.743Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pdmp3zj27v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pdmp3zj27v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Liang","last_name":"Lv","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jinyou","last_name":"Mo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumin","last_name":"Qing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuchao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Leijie","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Plasmid","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"99w5n6v67z","title":"Original data of \"Mitochondrial dysfunction activates ADAMTS-5 expression via mt-dsRNA-PKR-Spi-1 axis in osteoarthritic chondrocytes\"","description":"Original data of \"Mitochondrial dysfunction activates ADAMTS-5 expression via mt-dsRNA-PKR-Spi-1 axis in osteoarthritic chondrocytes\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-30T08:21:38.898Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/99w5n6v67z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/99w5n6v67z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Deqiang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Osteoarthritis","Mitochondrial Disorder","Articular Cartilage","Double-Stranded RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5thj4jyjkd","title":"AMP accumulation during mitochondrial stress induces transcription of cytosolic and mitochondrial protein synthesis components via NHR-180","description":"The mitochondrial unfolded protein response (UPRmt), which promotes mitochondrial biogenesis and proteostasis, is regulated by the transcription factor ATFS-1. In C. elegans, impaired atfs-1 expression perturbs mitochondrial function and slows development. Here, via an RNAi suppressor screen, we found that inhibition of the nuclear hormone receptor NHR-180 increases mitochondrial function and the rate of development in atfs-1(null) worms. NHR-180 is activated during mitochondrial dysfunction and induces transcription of genes required for protein synthesis on cytosolic and mitochondrial ribosomes. Importantly, the NHR-180 ligand binding domain interacts with AMP, which results in increased protein synthesis, potentially to promote mitochondrial biogenesis and recovery from transient mitochondrial perturbations.\nHowever, NHR-180 activation exacerbates the mitochondrial dysfunction caused by mutations in genes required for oxidative phosphorylation. Consistent with these findings, we found that inhibition of the S6 kinase homolog RSKS-1 also increased mitochondrial function in atfs-1(null) worms. Importantly, treatment with an S6 kinase inhibitor also increased respiration and mtDNA content in mammalian ATF5-knockout cells and OXPHOS-deficient models, suggesting that translation regulation is a conserved, targetable approach for restoring mitochondrial function in disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-28T09:50:52.919Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5thj4jyjkd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5thj4jyjkd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Avijit","last_name":"Mallick","orcid":"0000-0002-9423-7134"},{"first_name":"Cole ","last_name":"Haynes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6dyp7ds8bk","title":"Original data of \"Mitochondrial dysfunction activates ADAMTS-5 expression via mt-dsRNA-PKR-Spi-1 axis in osteoarthritic chondrocytes\"","description":"Original data of \"Mitochondrial dysfunction activates ADAMTS-5 expression via mt-dsRNA-PKR-Spi-1 axis in osteoarthritic chondrocytes\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-05T12:21:10.754Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6dyp7ds8bk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6dyp7ds8bk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Deqiang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrial RNA","Osteoarthritis","Mitochondrial Function"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w25cc5pv34","title":"Integrative study of skeletal muscle mitochondrial dysfunction in a murine pancreatic cancer-induced cachexia model","description":"Complete raw data set pertaining to the article Gicquel et al., Integrative study of skeletal muscle mitochondrial dysfunction in a murine pancreatic cancer-induced cachexia model. eLife. 2024.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-08T10:28:44.162Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w25cc5pv34","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w25cc5pv34"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tristan","last_name":"GICQUEL","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benoit","last_name":"Giannesini","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alice","last_name":"Carrier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer","Mitochondrial Disorder","Mitochondrial Metabolism","Muscle Exercise","Sarcopenia"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":129757,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":140,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":129757,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":129220,"restricted":64,"embargoed":473},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2151},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1439},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":600},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":412},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":359},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":288},{"id":"https://ro